            "Backup frequency and encryption uses frequency to decide how much recent work can be lost, which is the recovery point objective. Backup frequency and encryption uses encryption to decide whether a stolen or seized copy is readable. Backup frequency and encryption can run a full copy on a slow cadence because it takes time and space and restores as one set. Backup frequency and encryption can run an incremental job for changes since the last job of any kind, which is quick and small. Backup frequency and encryption must then restore the last full copy plus every incremental piece in order, or the result is incomplete. Backup frequency and encryption can run a differential job for changes since the last full copy, which grows until the next full. Backup frequency and encryption then restores with the last full copy plus only the latest differential. Backup frequency and encryption must not be described with the wrong one of those three words, because the restore steps change. Backup frequency and encryption sets a nightly job only if the business can lose a day's work. Backup frequency and encryption sets a tighter job if lunchtime work is too valuable to lose. Backup frequency and encryption does not, by itself, make restores fast, because speed is the recovery time objective and depends on size and location. Backup frequency and encryption encrypts the copy at rest and encrypts it in transit to the vault. Backup frequency and encryption stores keys apart from the media, or the thief who takes the box also takes the key. Backup frequency and encryption rotates keys with a procedure that still lets you read last year's retained set. Backup frequency and encryption tests a restore with the key the on-call engineer can actually obtain at night. Backup frequency and encryption avoids a key that lives only in the memory of one person. Backup frequency and encryption logs who can decrypt, because backup access is a quiet path to every record. Backup frequency and encryption does not leave a backup admin account shared among the whole team. Backup frequency and encryption aligns frequency with change rate: a quiet archive can be copied less often than a busy ledger. Backup frequency and encryption aligns encryption strength with the sensitivity of the data, and with the rule you must show an auditor. Backup frequency and encryption documents the algorithm and the key location in the runbook without pasting the key into the runbook. Backup frequency and encryption rechecks frequency after a business change, such as a new shift that enters orders all evening. Backup frequency and encryption rechecks encryption after a tool change, because a new agent can silently write clear copies. Backup frequency and encryption includes a job-success alert, because a frequent schedule that fails every night is not frequent. Backup frequency and encryption treats a failed job as data-loss risk equal to skipping the run. Backup frequency and encryption keeps an immutable window so ransomware cannot encrypt the only good generations. Backup frequency and encryption separates this topic from where the copy sits, which is the onsite versus offsite question. Backup frequency and encryption can still be nightly and encrypted and also trapped in the same burning building if no far copy exists. Backup frequency and encryption write both facts on the design: how often, and who can read a stolen copy. Backup frequency and encryption note, after a restore test, whether the key path delayed the clock."
